<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<p><strong>Key point:</strong> The class on sin can be the heaviest or the most liberating, depending on how you handle it. The emphasis should ALWAYS be on God&rsquo;s mercy. St. John Paul II said: &laquo;There is no sin that God cannot forgive.&raquo; Sin is real, but mercy is more real.</p>

<p><strong>Watch out for scrupulosity:</strong> Some people, especially the more sensitive, can become scrupulous (seeing mortal sin where there is none). Clarify that all three conditions must be met SIMULTANEOUSLY. Doubt about whether a sin is mortal already indicates it probably is not.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>&#x1F331; Seed (Ages 7-12)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Simplify: &laquo;Sin is when we say no to God, like when Peter denied Jesus. But Jesus always forgives.&raquo;</li>
  <li>Don&rsquo;t go into mortal/venial. Talk about &laquo;being naughty with God.&raquo;</li>
  <li>Activity: draw Jesus hugging someone who did something wrong.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1F33F; Sprout (Ages 13-17)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Connect to their experience: &laquo;Are there things you know are wrong but you do them anyway?&raquo;</li>
  <li>Talk about Confession as a second chance, not as punishment.</li>
  <li>Discuss how our culture minimizes sin (&laquo;it&rsquo;s not that bad&raquo;).</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1FAB4; Root (Adults new in faith)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>May carry shame from the past. Emphasis that God has already forgiven them.</li>
  <li>Help them make their first Confession if they haven&rsquo;t.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1F333; Tree (Formed adults)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Develop the theology of sin: &laquo;aversio a Deo et conversio ad creaturam&raquo; (St. Thomas).</li>
  <li>Discuss the relationship between personal sin and social/structural sin.</li>
  <li>Theological controversy about hell (CCC 1033-1037).</li>
</ul>

</section>

