<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<p><strong>1. &laquo;Can God forgive any sin?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>Yes. There is no sin God cannot forgive. The only &laquo;unforgivable&raquo; sin is blasphemy against the Holy Spirit (Mk 3:29), which the Fathers interpret as the deliberate refusal to accept God&rsquo;s mercy. God always forgives, but He respects our freedom to reject that forgiveness.</p>

<p><strong>2. &laquo;How do I know if a sin is mortal?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>If you had serious doubt while doing it, it probably wasn&rsquo;t. Consult it in Confession. God is more merciful than your scruples.</p>

<p><strong>3. &laquo;Are non-Catholics condemned?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>The Church teaches that those who through no fault of their own do not know the Gospel but seek God with a sincere heart can be saved (CCC 847-848). Salvation is possible for all, but the Church is the ordinary path.</p>

<p><strong>4. &laquo;Are there sins that cry to heaven?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>Yes, four: willful murder, sodomy, oppression of the poor, and defrauding workers of their wages (CCC 1867). All can be forgiven, but their gravity is such that they cry out for justice.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist&rsquo;s Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>
Merciful Lord,<br />
You who know the heart of every person,<br />
help me speak of sin with truth and love.<br />
May I not sow guilt where You sow hope.<br />
May I not minimize evil where You call to conversion.<br />
May I point to the wound while also pointing to the Healer.<br />
Amen.
</p>
</blockquote>

